= Sociology of knowledge — Technical =

The '''sociology of knowledge''' is the empirical and theoretical study of how knowledge claims, belief systems, classifications, intellectual styles, and epistemic institutions are conditioned by social structure and historical context. Its central problem is not whether society influences thought—it clearly does—but how such influence operates, at what level, and with what consequences for epistemic authority.

A technical account must avoid two symmetrical errors:

# '''asocial epistemology''' — treating knowledge production as if institutions and relationships do not matter;
# '''sociological reductionism''' — treating evidence and truth as nothing more than products of social power.


The sociology of knowledge connects social structure to the production and acceptance of [[Knowledge]] rather than treating belief as an isolated individual phenomenon. It therefore intersects with [[Intersubjectivity]], [[Group cognition]], and [[Collective intelligence]], while remaining a sociological inquiry into institutions, practices, and social location.

== Analytical model ==

Let a knowledge claim <math>c</math> be produced within social context

<math>S=(I,R,N,P,H),</math>

where:

* <math>I</math> = institutions;
* <math>R</math> = social relations;
* <math>N</math> = norms;
* <math>P</math> = power and resource distributions;
* <math>H</math> = historical context.

The probability that claim <math>c</math> is produced, accepted, or circulated can be modeled schematically as

<math>P(A_c=1)=F(E_c,S,X),</math>

where <math>E_c</math> is the available evidence and <math>X</math> includes other relevant variables.

The decomposition matters because social explanation and evidential support are distinct inputs.

== Durkheim and Mauss ==

Durkheim and Mauss examined how systems of classification can reflect collective organization.

Their work on classification suggested that conceptual categories are not merely individual mental products but can be related to social groupings and institutions.

Durkheim later extended sociological analysis to categories such as time and space in his sociology of religion.

Contemporary scholarship does not need to accept every historical ethnographic claim to recognize the methodological innovation: categories of thought can have social histories.

== Scheler ==

Max Scheler developed an early explicit sociology of knowledge.

His work distinguished forms and carriers of knowledge and examined how social conditions influence the selection and distribution of ideas.

Scheler did not simply argue that all knowledge is socially caused in the same manner. His philosophical commitments differed substantially from Mannheim's later formulation.

The label “sociology of knowledge” therefore contains internally diverse traditions from its beginning.

== Mannheim and relationism ==

Karl Mannheim's sociology of knowledge examines how social location affects thought.<ref name="mannheim">[https://www.routledge.com/Ideology-and-Utopia-An-Introduction-to-the-Sociology-of-Knowledge/Mannheim/p/book/9780415060547 Mannheim, K., ''Ideology and Utopia''.]</ref>

A key distinction is between:

* '''particular ideology''' — specific claims treated as distortions;
* '''total ideology''' — the wider thought structure associated with a group or historical position.

Mannheim's project creates a reflexive challenge. If social location conditions all thought, the sociologist's analysis is also conditioned.

His response is often described through '''relationism''': ideas should be understood relative to their social and historical genesis without concluding that all claims are equally valid.

This differs from simple relativism.

== Merton ==

Robert K. Merton critically systematized the sociology of knowledge and helped connect it to the sociology of science.

Mertonian analysis emphasizes institutions, roles, norms, and the organization of scientific communities.

His sociology of science examined how reward systems, priority, communication, and professional norms shape scientific activity.

This created a bridge between classical sociology of knowledge and later empirical studies of science.

== Berger and Luckmann ==

Berger and Luckmann redefine the field around “everything that passes for knowledge in society,” especially everyday common-sense knowledge.<ref name="berger">[https://books.google.com/books/about/The_Social_Construction_of_Reality.html?id=rGg9mT_JNIEC Berger, P. L. and Luckmann, T. (1966), ''The Social Construction of Reality''.]</ref>

Their model can be represented schematically as

<pre>
externalization
   ↓
habitualization
   ↓
institutionalization
   ↓
objectivation / legitimation
   ↓
internalization through socialization
</pre>

Individuals create recurring practices; practices become institutionalized; institutions appear as objective social reality; later individuals internalize them.

The cycle is historical and recursive.

== Institutionalization ==

Suppose repeated actions <math>a_t</math> become increasingly predictable:

<math>P(a_{t+1}=a^*\mid context)\rightarrow 1.</math>

Habitualization reduces decision cost.

When expectations become reciprocal and socially enforced, an institution can emerge.

Formal rules are not required. Institutions can be maintained by norms, sanctions, roles, language, and shared expectations.

The mathematical notation illustrates regularity; it is not Berger and Luckmann's formal model.

== Legitimation ==

Institutions require explanations that make them meaningful and justified.

Legitimation can include:

* practical explanations;
* moral rules;
* professional doctrines;
* legal reasoning;
* religious narratives;
* scientific classifications.

Higher-order legitimations can form symbolic universes that integrate many institutions into one coherent worldview.

A legitimation can be socially effective even if its factual premises are false.

== Socialization ==

Knowledge is transmitted through socialization.

'''Primary socialization''' refers to early incorporation into a social world.

'''Secondary socialization''' introduces specialized institutional roles and subworlds.

Professional education is a strong example: medicine, law, engineering, and science transmit vocabularies, procedures, role expectations, and standards of evidence.

The process creates epistemic competence but can also reproduce institutional blind spots.

== Classification systems ==

A classification system <math>C</math> maps observations to categories:

<math>C:X\rightarrow K.</math>

The sociology of knowledge asks:

* Who defined <math>K</math>?
* Which distinctions are included?
* Which observations become invisible?
* How is <math>C</math> enforced?
* What consequences follow from category assignment?

Examples include disease codes, census categories, scientific taxonomies, and legal statuses.

The social origin of a taxonomy does not imply that it lacks empirical utility.

Evaluation requires comparing classification performance to the phenomena and purposes involved.

== Sociology of scientific knowledge ==

The sociology of scientific knowledge extends sociological explanation to accepted as well as rejected scientific beliefs.

A strong methodological principle associated with some SSK traditions is '''symmetry''': true and false beliefs should both be open to sociological explanation rather than explaining error sociologically while treating truth as socially causeless.

This principle is methodological, not a demonstration that evidential differences are irrelevant.

A careful study can analyze why a scientific consensus formed while separately evaluating whether the evidence supports that consensus.

== Laboratory studies ==

Ethnographic and historical studies of laboratories examine:

* instrumentation;
* data selection;
* writing practices;
* negotiation;
* replication;
* expertise;
* material constraints.

Scientific facts require work to produce stable evidence and representations.

That does not imply the underlying physical phenomena are socially invented.

The important distinction is between constructing a reliable representation and constructing the object represented.

== Strong programme and controversy ==

Some sociology-of-scientific-knowledge traditions adopted strong naturalistic explanations of scientific belief.

Critics argue that radical sociological accounts can underplay evidence, material constraint, and the asymmetry between successful and failed theories.

The resulting debate helped clarify that social causation and epistemic evaluation operate at different explanatory levels.

== Ignorance and agnotology ==

Knowledge systems also produce ignorance.

Let an issue <math>q</math> have research attention <math>R(q)</math>. Low attention can result from:

* absent funding;
* institutional priorities;
* secrecy;
* methodological difficulty;
* political suppression.

Deliberate uncertainty production can also occur through selective communication or strategic research.

Studying ignorance is therefore part of understanding the social organization of knowledge.

== Expertise networks ==

Expertise is socially certified through institutions.

Let expert credibility be estimated from signals

<math>C_i=F(credentials,track\ record,peer\ evaluation,conflicts).</math>

No credential is infallible, but societies cannot independently verify every technical claim.

Expert systems therefore rely on second-order knowledge: knowing whom to trust.

Social epistemology analyzes when such trust is justified.<ref name="sep">[https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology-social/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Social Epistemology.”]</ref>

== Power and visibility ==

Institutions control epistemic resources.

A journal can allocate publication space. A funder can alter research priorities. A search engine can rank visibility.

If visibility probability is

<math>P(V_c=1)=G(Q_c,A_c,R_c),</math>

where <math>Q_c</math> is content quality, <math>A_c</math> authority, and <math>R_c</math> ranking/institutional factors, observed prominence does not identify <math>Q_c</math> alone.

This is important in digital knowledge systems.

== Network analysis ==

A scholarly network can be represented as

<math>G=(V,E)</math>

with authors, institutions, or papers as nodes and citation, collaboration, or affiliation as edges.

Network measures reveal structure:

* centrality;
* clusters;
* brokerage;
* assortativity.

But citation does not necessarily imply endorsement, and coauthorship does not reveal all intellectual influence.

Quantitative structure requires qualitative interpretation.

== Comparative institutional method ==

A strong research design compares institutions rather than assuming one causal explanation.

For example, compare two fields with different:

* funding regimes;
* peer-review systems;
* data-sharing norms;
* replication practices.

Outcomes could include correction latency, replication rate, concentration of authority, or participation.

Confounding factors must be addressed.

== Reflexivity ==

Researchers studying knowledge production are themselves embedded in knowledge institutions.

Reflexive practice can document:

* funding;
* disciplinary assumptions;
* coding decisions;
* access limitations;
* positionality;
* alternative interpretations.

Reflexivity improves transparency but does not replace evidence.

== Sociology and truth ==

Let <math>S(c)</math> denote the social explanation of claim <math>c</math> and <math>E(c)</math> its evidential support.

In general,

<math>S(c)\not\Rightarrow \neg E(c)</math>

and

<math>S(c)\not\Rightarrow E(c).</math>

Every human belief has a causal history. That fact alone cannot decide its truth.

This is the essential guard against the genetic fallacy.

== Artificial and platform-mediated knowledge ==

Digital platforms and AI systems introduce new social actors and infrastructures.

Questions include:

* how ranking shapes authority;
* how synthetic text changes publication volume;
* how automated moderation defines acceptable knowledge;
* how model provenance affects trust;
* how platform ownership concentrates epistemic power.

An AI model can influence socially accepted knowledge even if it has no institutional authority in the traditional sense.

Shared model dependence also makes apparently distributed discourse more correlated.

== Relation to collective intelligence ==

[[Collective intelligence]] concerns group problem-solving performance.

The sociology of knowledge asks how the group defines problems, establishes authority, and stabilizes claims.

A group can perform well while still reflecting social structures, and a socially legitimate consensus can perform poorly.

== Relation to knowledge ecosystem ==

A [[Knowledge ecosystem]] models actors, resources, infrastructure, and flows.

The sociology of knowledge adds analysis of social meaning, institutional authority, classification, power, and historical development.

== Research safeguards ==

Avoid:

* treating social construction as synonym for falsehood;
* reducing all beliefs to class or identity;
* assuming consensus equals truth;
* assuming dissent equals independence;
* interpreting citation as agreement;
* treating institutions as monolithic actors;
* using social explanation as evidence against a claim;
* ignoring material and empirical constraints.

